# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the tests (a build with WANT_DEBUG_TSAN sets them, see CMakeLists.txt).
#
# Qt is not built with ThreadSanitizer: a queued call (QMetaObject::invokeMethod with Qt::QueuedConnection) is made
# on one thread and run and destroyed on the receiver's, ordered by the locks of Qt's event queue, which ThreadSanitizer
# does not see. It then reports the call's captured values (e.g. a QString) as written on one thread and read on the
# other. Only the queued call's own object is left out here.
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject
#
# The same for any event that crosses threads through Qt's queue: one made on an audio thread (a queued signal,
# allocated from the real-time pool) and read by the main thread that receives it. The event's memory was last
# touched by the pool's bookkeeping under its lock on the audio thread, and the hand-over through Qt's queue is
# not seen.
race:QEvent::type
